+### Background utilities
+
+The background keys ship whole in the utilities layer from the
+`src/styles/utilities/_background.scss` partial: the `.bg-*` role, black, white, body, and
+transparent fills, the `.bg-opacity-*` steps, the `.bg-*-subtle` tiers, and the `.bg-gradient`
+class.
+
+Each role fill reads its role's channel alias, such as the `--bs-primary-rgb` property for the
+`.bg-primary` class, at the alpha the `--bs-bg-opacity` local holds, and the fill's own rule writes
+the `1` value into that local. So a retuned channel alias moves its fill, and an opacity step on the
+same element sets the fill's alpha: an element carrying the `.bg-success` and `.bg-opacity-25`
+classes paints the success role at a quarter of its opacity. A step alone paints nothing, and a step
+on an ancestor reaches no nested fill, because each fill sets the local in its own rule. The fills
+walk the roles Bootstrap names, so Veneer's own `tertiary` role writes no `.bg-tertiary` class. The
+`.bg-body`, `.bg-body-secondary`, and `.bg-body-tertiary` classes read the body surface channel
+aliases, and each `.bg-*-subtle` tier reads its role's `--bs-*-bg-subtle` alias. The theme
+re-declares those aliases in each mode, so the same class paints the dark surface inside a
+`data-bs-theme="dark"` island. The `.bg-gradient` class lays the image the `--bs-gradient` alias
+holds over whatever fill the element carries.
+
+The partial writes every fill, tier, and gradient through the `utility` mixin that § Styles
+describes, and the opacity steps through the `utility-variable` mixin. So each of those
+declarations carries the `!important` flag the release writes, and the opacity local and the
+opacity steps are normal declarations, as the release writes them: an unlayered rule of your own
+that sets the `--bs-bg-opacity` property reaches a fill, and one that sets the `background-color`
+property does not. The partial writes the entries in the release's map order, so every opacity step
+follows the fills it dims. No background key writes a breakpoint infix. § Styles shows the escape
+from an important utility inside the utilities layer.
+
+The `tests/src/styles/utilities/background.test.ts` proof reads each fill against its channel alias
+and a retune of every alias, the absence of a `tertiary` class, each opacity step and a step alone,
+the local on a nested fill, the subtle and body tiers in a light and a dark island, the gradient and
+its retune, the density factor, each class written once at no infix, the `.bg-danger` and
+`.bg-opacity-50` composition and the `.bg-warning-subtle` class read at every boundary and one pixel
+below it, the priority over a later unlayered rule, and the escape inside the utilities layer. The
+background keys carry no recorded departure.
+
+### Border utilities
+
+The border and rounded keys ship whole in the utilities layer from the
+`src/styles/utilities/_border.scss` partial: the `.border` class and its `.border-0` removal, the
+`.border-top`, `.border-end`, `.border-bottom`, and `.border-start` sides with their `-0` removals,
+the role, black, white, and subtle border colors, the `.border-1` to `.border-5` widths, the
+`.border-opacity-*` steps, and the `.rounded` class with its `.rounded-top`, `.rounded-end`,
+`.rounded-bottom`, and `.rounded-start` sides, each at the `0` to `5`, `circle`, and `pill` steps.
+
+The `.border` class draws the `--bs-border-width`, `--bs-border-style`, and `--bs-border-color`
+aliases. Those read the `--vn-border-width` token, the `--vn-border-style` token, and the border
+color the theme re-declares in each mode, so a retuned width token widens every default border and
+a dark island draws the dark border color. Each side class sets the physical side the release sets:
+the `.border-end` class sets the `border-right` property and the `.border-start` class the
+`border-left` property. The side entries follow the `border` entry in the release's map, so an
+element carrying the `.border` and `.border-top-0` classes draws every side but the top, whatever
+order the classes are written in. A border color reads its role's channel alias at the alpha the
+`--bs-border-opacity` local holds, as a background fill reads its own, and an opacity step sets that
+alpha. The widths stay literal, because no published Veneer token carries them.
+
+The rounded steps read the `--bs-border-radius-*` aliases, which read the `--vn-radius-*` tokens, so
+the `--vn-factor-radius` token scales the `.rounded` class and the `1` to `5` steps: the `2` factor
+doubles the `.rounded-2` class from 6px to 12px. The `.rounded` class writes the `2` step's radius.
+Each side class rounds two corners, and the side entries follow the `rounded` entry in the map, so
+an element carrying the `.rounded-3` and `.rounded-top-0` classes squares its top corners and keeps
+its bottom ones. The release writes the rounded entries later in its map than the border entries; no
+utility between them sets a radius, so the partial writes them beside the border entries, and the
+placement moves no resolution.
+
+The partial writes every border, color, width, and radius through the `utility` mixin, so each
+declaration carries the `!important` flag the release writes, and it writes the opacity steps
+through the `utility-variable` mixin, so they and the opacity local stay normal. No border or
+rounded key writes a breakpoint infix. § Styles shows the escape from an important utility inside
+the utilities layer.
+
+The `tests/src/styles/utilities/border.test.ts` proof reads the default border against its aliases
+and a retuned width token, each side and each removal, each color against its channel alias and a
+retune of every alias, the absence of a `tertiary` class, each opacity step and a step alone, the
+default and subtle colors in a light and a dark island, each width over a whole border, each radius
